{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779395894,"nonce":"oPhlA7slGRWoIzZZXppf/xWJP4c=","title":"Studio schema constraint vocabulary for dimension propagation","message":"Parent: 0258fac\n\n## Summary\nDefine the schema vocabulary that lets Studio explain and infer dimensions instead of only comparing opaque dtype/rank/shape fields. This is the contract slice for schema-driven dimension propagation; it should land before the UI or auto-sync behavior depends on it.\n\n## UX decision this supports\nPort and Task Data surfaces should show compact schema summaries such as `float32 [time, 2]`, `vector [4]`, or `state [?]`, with richer provenance in tooltips/inspectors. Users should be able to tell whether a dimension is declared by a component, inferred from task data, derived from a param such as `Network.input_size`, or unknown. This should not become an always-visible metadata dump on the canvas.\n\n## Required contract decisions\n- Keep `ValueSchema.shape` as the user-facing shape list, allowing concrete integers, symbolic axes such as `time`/`channels`, and wildcard dimensions such as `*`/`any`/`null`.\n- Distinguish semantic value category from concrete element dtype. Existing records currently mix `vector`, `state`, `scalar`, and `float32` in `dtype`; this slice should preserve compatibility but add normalized metadata or helper accessors so display/validation can say both \"vector-like\" and \"float32\" when both are known.\n- Add dimension provenance metadata for params that own port sizes. At minimum cover:\n  - `Network.input` last axis -\u003e `nodes.\u003cid\u003e.params.input_size`\n  - `Network.output` last axis -\u003e `nodes.\u003cid\u003e.params.out_size`\n  - Network internal `cell.params.input_size` mirrors outer `Network.input_size`\n  - Network internal `readout.params.output_size` mirrors outer `Network.out_size`\n  - `Linear.input`/`Linear.output` -\u003e `input_size`/`output_size` inside Network subgraphs\n- Add a per-port \"sample view\" / \"binding view\" concept so trajectory Task Data with shape `[time, channels]` can bind to per-step vector ports with shape `[channels]`.\n- Add concat semantics for `Mux`: output last-axis dimension is the sum of bound input sample-view dimensions when all are known; unknown inputs make the output dimension unknown with explanation.\n- Add required mechanics output constraints for this item: `TwoLinkArm.force` and `PointMass.force` imply 2-D force; `Arm6MuscleRigidTendon.excitation` implies 6-D excitation; `PointMass8MuscleRelu.excitation` implies `2 * n_pairs` when `n_pairs` is known.\n\n## Out of scope\n- No automatic parameter mutation in this issue.\n- No broad `.eqx` compatibility work.\n- No probe/intervention binding model changes beyond using the same normalized schema helpers later.\n\n## Implementation notes\nBackend and frontend projection must agree. The likely touchpoints are `feedbax/studio_schema.py`, `feedbax/web/services/component_registry.py`, `web/src/types/workspace.ts`, `web/src/types/components.ts`, and `web/src/features/schema/project.ts`. Prefer shared, explicit helper functions over duplicating shape-normalization rules ad hoc.\n\n## Done criteria\n- Schema registries expose enough information to explain shape/rank/dtype/units/frame and source/provenance for the covered ports and Task Data.\n- Task Data binding compatibility compares trajectory sample views against graph ports instead of blindly comparing full trajectory rank.\n- Mux output dimensions can be inferred from input dimensions when known.\n- Backend and frontend tests cover matching, unknown, wildcard, trajectory-to-port, mux concat, and mechanics constraints.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}